; 25-compute-with-vectors-and-matrices.kurt
;
; Compute with vectors and matrices
;
; `load matrix` gives literals of numbers: `[1, 2, 3]` is a row, `[[1, 2], [3, 4]]` a matrix
; written row by row, `[[1], [3]]` a column. With `calc on`, Kurt computes them exactly, like
; numbers (lesson 10): `+`, `-`, `·` (`\cdot`), `transpose`, `det`.

load matrix
calc on

[1, 2] + [3, 4] = [4, 6]
2 · [1, 2, 3] = [2, 4, 6]
[[1, 2], [3, 4]] · [[1], [1]] = [[3], [7]]
transpose [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]] = [[1, 4], [2, 5], [3, 6]]
det [[1, 2], [3, 4]] = -2

; Written over several lines, each line inside the brackets is a row -- as on paper:

[1, 2
 3, 4] · [1
          1] = [[3], [7]]

; If the shapes don't fit, there is no value: `[1, 2] + [1, 2, 3]` stays as it is, and a claim
; about its value can't be derived.
expect "ProofError"
    [1, 2] + [1, 2, 3] = [2, 4, 3]

; matrix.kurt has its own `+` and `·`, so it isn't loaded together with `numbers` (lesson 18). It
; computes with literals; for laws about matrices written with variables, see
; proofs/mafi1/matrices.kurt.
